# dot_construction_reject.tkr — the §4.1 `.{ … }` REJECT host. Two illegal uses live one per namespace
# under src/, and the single failing build reports every diagnostic it finds (the compiler does not
# stop at the first) — one per scenario below. `.{ … }` is legal ONLY in a slot that supplies a target
# type; it never infers by back-flow, so a `.{ … }` with no target in its immediate slot is a compile
# error. It has no successful build to sanitize/run.

Feature: §4.1 `.{ … }` reject — the compiler rejects a `.{ … }` with no target type

  # (no_target) a `.{ … }` bound to an UN-annotated `var` has no target in its slot (§4.1) — the
  # target comes only from the immediate slot, never by back-flow.
  Scenario: no_target — a `.{ … }` in an un-annotated binding is rejected
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "needs a known target type"

  # (scrutinee) a `.{ … }` as an `if` subject has no target type — the unconditional `.{ … }` parse
  # opens no semantic hole (a mis-placed `.{ … }` still fails the type-check).
  Scenario: scrutinee — a `.{ … }` as an `if` scrutinee is rejected
    When compilation fails
    Then diagnostic = "needs a known target type"
